#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# fourier_transform

#% text
# `fourier_transform(f, t, w)` gives the Fourier transform `1/sqrt(2 pi) integrate(f*exp(im*w*t), [t, -inf, inf])` of `f`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Factors `unit_step(t - a)` and `unit_step(b - t)` become the bounds of the integral.

#% item
# Gaussians `exp(-a t^2 + b t + c)` (with `re(a) > 0`) have a closed form.

#% item
# Otherwise the integral is left to `integrate`; when it gives no value the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# `inverse_fourier_transform` uses the kernel `exp(-im*w*t)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A rectangular pulse:

#% cell
fourier_transform(unit_step(t)*unit_step(1 - t), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(1im/w + -1im*exp(1im*w)/w)*2^(1/2)*pi^(-1/2)

#% text
# A Gaussian:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A symmetric pulse:

#% cell
fourier_transform(unit_step(t + 1)*unit_step(1 - t), t, w)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(-1im*exp(1im*w)/w + 1im*exp(-1im*w)/w)*2^(1/2)*pi^(-1/2)

#% text
# A shifted Gaussian:

#% cell
fourier_transform(exp(-(t - 1)^2/2), t, w)
#% output
#= exp(-1/2 + (1/2)*(1 + 1im*w)^2)

#% text
# A list:

#% cell
fourier_transform([exp(-t^2), exp(-2*t^2)], t, w)
#% output
#= [(1/2)*2^(1/2)*exp((-1/4)*w^2), (1/2)*exp((-1/8)*w^2)]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The inverse transform gives the function back:

#% cell
inverse_fourier_transform(fourier_transform(exp(-t^2), t, w), w, t)
#% output
#= exp(-t^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `inverse_fourier_transform`, `integrate`, `unit_step`
